WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.786 --> 00:00:16.716
There is some medical news
that nobody, absolutely nobody,

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.161
is prepared to hear.

00:00:18.817 --> 00:00:20.221
I certainly wasn't.

00:00:20.832 --> 00:00:24.473
It was three years ago
that I got a call in my office

00:00:24.497 --> 00:00:27.181
with the test results of a recent scan.

00:00:27.205 --> 00:00:31.116
I was 35 and finally living
the life I wanted.

00:00:31.711 --> 00:00:33.805
I married my high school sweetheart

00:00:33.829 --> 00:00:37.534
and had finally gotten pregnant
after years of infertility.

00:00:38.344 --> 00:00:40.452
And then suddenly we had a Zach,

00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:44.959
a perfect one-year-old boy/dinosaur,

00:00:44.983 --> 00:00:46.333
depending on his mood.

00:00:46.944 --> 00:00:50.029
And having a Zach suited me perfectly.

00:00:51.168 --> 00:00:55.191
I had gotten the first job
I applied for in academia,

00:00:55.215 --> 00:00:57.903
land of a thousand crushed dreams.

00:00:58.601 --> 00:00:59.888
And there I was,

00:00:59.912 --> 00:01:01.935
working at my dream job

00:01:01.959 --> 00:01:03.441
with my little baby

00:01:03.465 --> 00:01:06.213
and the man I had imported from Canada.

00:01:06.237 --> 00:01:07.633
(Laughter)

00:01:07.657 --> 00:01:10.635
But a few months before,
I'd started feeling pain in my stomach

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:13.206
and had gone to every expert
to find out why.

00:01:13.779 --> 00:01:15.068
No one could tell me.

00:01:15.444 --> 00:01:17.944
And then, out of the blue,

00:01:17.968 --> 00:01:21.515
some physician's assistant
called me at work

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:24.328
to tell me that I had stage IV cancer,

00:01:24.352 --> 00:01:27.400
and that I was going to need
to come to the hospital right away.

00:01:28.239 --> 00:01:31.245
And all I could think of to say was,

00:01:31.269 --> 00:01:32.789
"But I have a son.

00:01:33.945 --> 00:01:35.919
I can't end.

00:01:35.943 --> 00:01:38.225
This world can't end.

00:01:38.249 --> 00:01:40.479
It has just begun."

00:01:41.458 --> 00:01:44.101
And then I called my husband,
and he rushed to find me

00:01:44.125 --> 00:01:46.935
and I said all the true things
that I have known.

00:01:46.959 --> 00:01:50.019
I said, "I have loved you forever,

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.707
I have loved you forever.

00:01:51.731 --> 00:01:53.715
I am so sorry.

00:01:53.739 --> 00:01:55.797
Please take care of our son."

00:01:56.806 --> 00:01:59.535
And then as I began
the walk to the hospital,

00:01:59.559 --> 00:02:02.502
it crossed my mind for the first time,

00:02:02.526 --> 00:02:05.261
"Oh. How ironic."

00:02:05.285 --> 00:02:08.725
I had just written
a book called "Blessed."

00:02:09.097 --> 00:02:11.097
(Laughter)

00:02:12.077 --> 00:02:13.329
I am a historian

00:02:13.353 --> 00:02:16.913
and an expert in the idea
that good things happen to good people.

00:02:17.373 --> 00:02:21.639
I research a form of Christianity
nicknamed "the prosperity gospel,"

00:02:21.663 --> 00:02:25.859
for its very bold promise
that God wants you to prosper.

00:02:25.883 --> 00:02:29.642
I never considered myself
a follower of the prosperity gospel.

00:02:29.666 --> 00:02:31.612
I was simply an observer.

00:02:32.134 --> 00:02:35.801
The prosperity gospel believes
that God wants to reward you

00:02:35.825 --> 00:02:37.935
if you have the right kind of faith.

00:02:37.959 --> 00:02:40.325
If you're good and faithful,

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:42.558
God will give you health and wealth

00:02:42.582 --> 00:02:44.405
and boundless happiness.

00:02:44.817 --> 00:02:46.292
Life is like a boomerang:

00:02:46.316 --> 00:02:47.625
if you're good,

00:02:47.649 --> 00:02:50.221
good things will always come back to you.

00:02:50.245 --> 00:02:53.649
Think positively. Speak positively.

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:56.870
Nothing is impossible if you believe.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.556
I got interested in this
very American theology

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.207
when I was 18 or so,

00:03:03.231 --> 00:03:07.664
and by 25 I was traveling the country
interviewing its celebrities.

00:03:08.148 --> 00:03:11.854
I spent a decade talking to televangelists

00:03:11.878 --> 00:03:14.914
with spiritual guarantees
for divine money.

00:03:15.501 --> 00:03:19.975
I interviewed countless megachurch pastors
with spectacular hair

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:22.678
about how they live their best lives now.

00:03:23.545 --> 00:03:26.152
I visited with people
in hospital waiting rooms

00:03:26.176 --> 00:03:27.704
and plush offices.

00:03:28.221 --> 00:03:30.730
I held hands with people in wheelchairs,

00:03:30.754 --> 00:03:32.252
praying to be cured.

00:03:33.252 --> 00:03:37.957
I earned my reputation
as destroyer of family vacations

00:03:37.981 --> 00:03:42.216
for always insisting on being dropped off
at the fanciest megachurch in town.

00:03:42.653 --> 00:03:45.651
If there was a river
running through the sanctuary,

00:03:45.675 --> 00:03:48.749
an eagle flying freely in the auditorium,

00:03:48.773 --> 00:03:51.593
or an enormous spinning golden globe,

00:03:51.617 --> 00:03:52.845
I was there.

00:03:54.199 --> 00:03:57.879
When I first started studying this,
the whole idea of being "blessed"

00:03:57.903 --> 00:03:59.562
wasn't what it is today.

00:04:00.062 --> 00:04:02.145
It was not, like it is now,

00:04:02.169 --> 00:04:05.084
an entire line of "#blessed" home goods.

00:04:05.758 --> 00:04:10.906
It was not yet a flood of "#blessed"
vanity license plates and T-shirts

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.564
and neon wall art.

00:04:13.275 --> 00:04:18.631
I had no idea that "blessed" would become
one of the most common cultural cliches,

00:04:18.655 --> 00:04:21.572
one of the most used
hashtags on Instagram,

00:04:21.596 --> 00:04:24.171
to celebrate barely there bikini shots,

00:04:24.195 --> 00:04:26.635
as if to say, "I am so blessed.

00:04:26.659 --> 00:04:28.928
Thank you, Jesus, for this body."

00:04:28.952 --> 00:04:31.465
(Laughter)

00:04:32.233 --> 00:04:36.065
I had not yet fully grasped
the way that the prosperity gospel

00:04:36.089 --> 00:04:39.006
had become the great civil religion,

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.530
offering another transcendent account

00:04:41.554 --> 00:04:43.683
of the core of the American Dream.

00:04:44.176 --> 00:04:47.684
Rather than worshipping
the founding of America itself,

00:04:47.708 --> 00:04:50.581
the prosperity gospel
worshipped Americans.

00:04:51.027 --> 00:04:54.187
It deifies and ritualizes their hungers,

00:04:54.211 --> 00:04:56.865
their hard work and moral fiber.

00:04:58.305 --> 00:05:01.315
Americans believe in a gospel of optimism,

00:05:01.339 --> 00:05:03.257
and they are their own proof.

00:05:04.459 --> 00:05:06.163
But despite telling myself,

00:05:06.187 --> 00:05:09.475
"I'm just studying this stuff,
I'm nothing like them,"

00:05:09.499 --> 00:05:11.177
when I got my diagnosis,

00:05:11.201 --> 00:05:15.399
I suddenly understood
how deeply invested I was

00:05:15.423 --> 00:05:18.093
in my own Horatio Alger theology.

00:05:19.178 --> 00:05:22.389
If you live in this culture,
whether you are religious or not,

00:05:22.413 --> 00:05:26.216
it is extremely difficult
to avoid falling into the trap

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.501
of believing that virtue
and success go hand in hand.

00:05:31.485 --> 00:05:33.993
The more I stared down my diagnosis,

00:05:34.017 --> 00:05:37.397
the more I recognized
that I had my own quiet version

00:05:37.421 --> 00:05:40.526
of the idea that good things
happen to good people.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:42.306
Aren't I good?

00:05:42.744 --> 00:05:44.560
Aren't I special somehow?

00:05:45.144 --> 00:05:47.946
I have committed zero homicides

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.135
to date.

00:05:49.159 --> 00:05:51.658
(Laughter)

00:05:51.682 --> 00:05:53.618
(Applause)

00:05:53.642 --> 00:05:55.425
So why is this happening to me?

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:58.832
I wanted God to make me good

00:05:58.856 --> 00:06:02.816
and to reward my faith with just a few
shining awards along the way.

00:06:03.377 --> 00:06:05.631
OK, like, a lot of shining awards.

00:06:05.655 --> 00:06:06.663
(Laughter)

00:06:06.687 --> 00:06:09.494
I believed that hardships
were only detours

00:06:09.518 --> 00:06:13.079
on what I was certain would be
my long, long life.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:18.371
As is this case with many of us,
it's a mindset that served me well.

00:06:19.024 --> 00:06:22.146
The gospel of success drove me to achieve,

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:23.740
to dream big,

00:06:23.764 --> 00:06:25.213
to abandon fear.

00:06:26.183 --> 00:06:28.655
It was a mindset that served me well

00:06:28.679 --> 00:06:30.252
until it didn't,

00:06:30.276 --> 00:06:34.237
until I was confronted with something
I couldn't manage my way out of;

00:06:35.078 --> 00:06:37.720
until I found myself
saying into the phone,

00:06:37.744 --> 00:06:39.273
"But I have a son,"

00:06:39.776 --> 00:06:42.792
because it was all
I could think of to say.

00:06:45.411 --> 00:06:47.946
That was the most difficult
moment to accept:

00:06:48.637 --> 00:06:51.880
the phone call, the walk to the hospital,

00:06:51.904 --> 00:06:55.603
when I realized that my own
personal prosperity gospel

00:06:55.627 --> 00:06:56.816
had failed me.

00:06:57.673 --> 00:07:02.628
Anything I thought was good
or special about me could not save me --

00:07:02.652 --> 00:07:05.436
my hard work, my personality,

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.141
my humor, my perspective.

00:07:08.914 --> 00:07:12.763
I had to face the fact that my life
is built with paper walls,

00:07:12.787 --> 00:07:14.718
and so is everyone else's.

00:07:16.821 --> 00:07:20.926
It is a hard thought to accept
that we are all a breath away

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:24.731
from a problem that could
destroy something irreplaceable

00:07:24.755 --> 00:07:26.867
or alter our lives completely.

00:07:27.644 --> 00:07:30.924
We know that in life
there are befores and afters.

00:07:31.743 --> 00:07:36.064
I am asked all the time to say
that I would never go back,

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:38.595
or that I've gained
so much in perspective.

00:07:39.074 --> 00:07:40.883
And I tell them no,

00:07:40.907 --> 00:07:42.851
before was better.

00:07:45.793 --> 00:07:48.103
A few months after I got sick,
I wrote about this

00:07:48.127 --> 00:07:51.106
and then I sent it off to an editor
at the "New York Times."

00:07:51.742 --> 00:07:55.939
In retrospect, taking one of the most
vulnerable moments of your life

00:07:55.963 --> 00:07:57.493
and turning into an op-ed

00:07:57.517 --> 00:08:00.307
is not an amazing way
to feel less vulnerable.

00:08:00.331 --> 00:08:01.817
(Laughter)

00:08:01.841 --> 00:08:04.616
I got thousands of letters and emails.

00:08:04.943 --> 00:08:06.496
I still get them every day.

00:08:07.274 --> 00:08:09.901
I think it is because
of the questions I asked.

00:08:10.321 --> 00:08:14.921
I asked: How do you live
without quite so many reasons

00:08:14.945 --> 00:08:16.648
for the bad things that happen?

00:08:17.124 --> 00:08:22.386
I asked: Would it be better to live
without outrageous formulas

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.894
for why people deserve what they get?

00:08:26.093 --> 00:08:29.340
And what was so funny
and so terrible was, of course,

00:08:29.364 --> 00:08:31.606
I thought I asked people to simmer down

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:34.393
on needing an explanation
for the bad things that happened.

00:08:34.417 --> 00:08:37.048
So what did thousands of readers do?

00:08:37.072 --> 00:08:41.357
Yeah, they wrote to defend the idea
that there had to be a reason

00:08:41.381 --> 00:08:43.093
for what happened to me.

00:08:43.117 --> 00:08:45.904
And they really want me
to understand the reason.

00:08:46.438 --> 00:08:50.935
People want me to reassure them
that my cancer is all part of a plan.

00:08:51.808 --> 00:08:55.229
A few letters even suggested
it was God's plan that I get cancer

00:08:55.253 --> 00:08:57.579
so I could help people
by writing about it.

00:08:58.669 --> 00:09:01.650
People are certain
it is a test of my character

00:09:01.674 --> 00:09:03.972
or proof of something terrible I've done.

00:09:04.744 --> 00:09:07.774
They want me to know without a doubt

00:09:07.798 --> 00:09:11.631
that there is a hidden logic
to this seeming chaos.

00:09:12.302 --> 00:09:13.933
They tell my husband,

00:09:13.957 --> 00:09:15.958
while I'm still in the hospital,

00:09:15.982 --> 00:09:18.465
that everything happens for a reason,

00:09:18.489 --> 00:09:21.257
and then stammer awkwardly when he says,

00:09:21.281 --> 00:09:22.570
"I'd love to hear it.

00:09:23.111 --> 00:09:26.007
I'd love to hear the reason
my wife is dying."

00:09:27.636 --> 00:09:28.802
And I get it.

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:31.109
We all want reasons.

00:09:31.133 --> 00:09:32.839
We want formulas

00:09:32.863 --> 00:09:36.438
to predict whether
our hard work will pay off,

00:09:36.462 --> 00:09:40.600
whether our love and support
will always make our partners happy

00:09:40.624 --> 00:09:42.188
and our kids love us.

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:46.371
We want to live in a world
in which not one ounce

00:09:46.395 --> 00:09:52.039
of our hard work or our pain
or our deepest hopes will be for nothing.

00:09:52.793 --> 00:09:56.696
We want to live in a world
in which nothing is lost.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.892
But what I have learned
in living with stage IV cancer

00:10:02.916 --> 00:10:05.557
is that there is no easy correlation

00:10:05.581 --> 00:10:08.142
between how hard I try

00:10:08.166 --> 00:10:09.815
and the length of my life.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:14.920
In the last three years,
I've experienced more pain and trauma

00:10:14.944 --> 00:10:17.105
than I ever thought I could survive.

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:21.454
I realized the other day that I've had
so many abdominal surgeries

00:10:21.478 --> 00:10:23.781
that I'm on my fifth belly button,

00:10:23.805 --> 00:10:26.183
and this last one is my least favorite.

00:10:26.207 --> 00:10:28.021
(Laughter)

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:32.865
But at the same time,
I've experienced love,

00:10:32.889 --> 00:10:34.896
so much love,

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:37.178
love I find hard to explain.

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:40.595
The other day, I was reading the findings

00:10:40.619 --> 00:10:43.811
of the Near Death Experience
Research Foundation,

00:10:43.835 --> 00:10:45.917
and yes, there is such a thing.

00:10:45.941 --> 00:10:48.537
People were interviewed
about their brushes with death

00:10:48.561 --> 00:10:50.307
in all kinds of circumstances:

00:10:50.331 --> 00:10:52.799
car accidents, labor and delivery,

00:10:52.823 --> 00:10:54.256
suicides.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:56.637
And many reported the same odd thing:

00:10:57.429 --> 00:10:58.603
love.

00:10:59.127 --> 00:11:01.844
I'm sure I would have ignored it
if it hadn't reminded me

00:11:01.868 --> 00:11:03.384
of something I had experienced,

00:11:03.408 --> 00:11:06.197
something I felt
uncomfortable telling anyone:

00:11:06.763 --> 00:11:09.617
that when I was sure
that I was going to die,

00:11:09.641 --> 00:11:11.437
I didn't feel angry.

00:11:12.626 --> 00:11:14.187
I felt loved.

00:11:15.123 --> 00:11:18.619
It was one of the most surreal things
I have experienced.

00:11:18.643 --> 00:11:22.736
In a time in which I should have
felt abandoned by God,

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:25.054
I was not reduced to ashes.

00:11:25.598 --> 00:11:27.943
I felt like I was floating,

00:11:27.967 --> 00:11:29.731
floating on the love and prayers

00:11:29.755 --> 00:11:33.016
of all those who hummed
around me like worker bees,

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:35.837
bringing me notes and socks and flowers

00:11:35.861 --> 00:11:39.100
and quilts embroidered
with words of encouragement.

00:11:39.707 --> 00:11:41.879
But when they sat beside me,

00:11:41.903 --> 00:11:43.826
my hand in their hands,

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:47.468
my own suffering began to feel
like it had revealed to me

00:11:47.492 --> 00:11:48.951
the suffering of others.

00:11:49.733 --> 00:11:52.707
I was entering a world
of people just like me,

00:11:53.572 --> 00:11:55.824
people stumbling around in the debris

00:11:55.848 --> 00:11:58.664
of dreams they thought
they were entitled to

00:11:58.688 --> 00:12:01.338
and plans they didn't
realize they had made.

00:12:02.126 --> 00:12:06.358
It was a feeling of being more connected,
somehow, with other people,

00:12:06.382 --> 00:12:08.645
experiencing the same situation.

00:12:09.304 --> 00:12:12.032
And that feeling
stayed with me for months.

00:12:12.493 --> 00:12:14.368
In fact, I'd grown so accustomed to it

00:12:14.392 --> 00:12:17.642
that I started to panic
at the prospect of losing it.

00:12:17.666 --> 00:12:23.908
So I began to ask friends, theologians,
historians, nuns I liked,

00:12:23.932 --> 00:12:27.543
"What I am I going to do
when that loving feeling is gone?"

00:12:28.143 --> 00:12:30.445
And they knew exactly
what I was talking about,

00:12:30.469 --> 00:12:32.756
because they had either
experienced it themselves

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:35.810
or they'd read about it
in great works of Christian theology.

00:12:35.834 --> 00:12:37.113
And they said,

00:12:37.137 --> 00:12:39.176
"Yeah, it'll go.

00:12:39.872 --> 00:12:41.348
The feelings will go.

00:12:41.817 --> 00:12:44.784
And there will be no formula
for how to get it back."

00:12:45.672 --> 00:12:48.190
But they offered me
this little piece of reassurance,

00:12:48.214 --> 00:12:49.576
and I clung to it.

00:12:50.375 --> 00:12:51.545
They said,

00:12:51.569 --> 00:12:54.663
"When the feelings recede like the tides,

00:12:54.687 --> 00:12:56.480
they will leave an imprint."

00:12:57.806 --> 00:12:59.272
And they do.

00:12:59.887 --> 00:13:02.970
And it is not proof of anything,

00:13:03.906 --> 00:13:05.740
and it is nothing to boast about.

00:13:05.764 --> 00:13:07.395
It was just a gift.

00:13:07.419 --> 00:13:10.284
So I can't respond to
the thousands of emails I get

00:13:10.308 --> 00:13:12.992
with my own five-step plan
to divine health

00:13:13.016 --> 00:13:15.126
and magical floating feelings.

00:13:16.023 --> 00:13:22.006
I see that the world is jolted by events
that are wonderful and terrible,

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:24.380
gorgeous and tragic.

00:13:25.191 --> 00:13:27.475
I can't reconcile the contradiction,

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:31.752
except that I am beginning to believe
that these opposites

00:13:31.776 --> 00:13:34.239
do not cancel each other out.

00:13:35.047 --> 00:13:37.496
Life is so beautiful,

00:13:37.520 --> 00:13:39.520
and life is so hard.

00:13:42.372 --> 00:13:45.063
Today, I am doing quite well.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:49.061
The immunotherapy drugs
appear to be working,

00:13:49.085 --> 00:13:51.846
and we are watching
and waiting with scans.

00:13:52.709 --> 00:13:54.906
I hope I will live a long time.

00:13:55.990 --> 00:14:00.224
I hope I will live long enough
to embarrass my son

00:14:00.248 --> 00:14:03.192
and to watch my husband
lose his beautiful hair.

00:14:04.027 --> 00:14:05.476
And I think I might.

00:14:06.161 --> 00:14:08.310
But I am learning to live

00:14:08.334 --> 00:14:10.121
and to love

00:14:10.145 --> 00:14:12.363
without counting the cost,

00:14:12.387 --> 00:14:16.588
without reasons and assurances
that nothing will be lost.

00:14:17.143 --> 00:14:19.304
Life will break your heart,

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:22.763
and life may take everything you have

00:14:22.787 --> 00:14:24.524
and everything you hope for.

00:14:25.063 --> 00:14:29.309
But there is one kind
of prosperity gospel that I believe in.

00:14:29.868 --> 00:14:32.236
I believe that in the darkness,

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:33.791
even there,

00:14:33.815 --> 00:14:35.613
there will be beauty,

00:14:35.637 --> 00:14:37.318
and there will be love.

00:14:37.342 --> 00:14:39.825
And every now and then,

00:14:39.849 --> 00:14:42.886
it will feel like more than enough.

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:45.191
Thank you.

00:14:45.215 --> 00:14:48.650
(Applause)

